{
  "content": "**Updated Cheatsheet**\n\n### 1) Build the spec strictly from user messages\nStart by reconstructing the task only from what the user has explicitly said. Treat assistant guesses, plausible completions, and inferred details as non-spec until a user turn confirms them. Keep the spec minimal but complete: include every explicit constraint, output field, scope boundary, ordering requirement, and exclusion the user has stated.\n\n### 2) Update the spec incrementally as user turns accumulate\nIn sharded or incremental disclosure, do not freeze an early interpretation. Add each new user constraint monotonically and re-check earlier assistant behavior against the expanded spec. The right comparison target is the best available cumulative user-grounded spec, not just the latest turn in isolation.\n\n### 3) Defer final judgment until all revealed constraints are integrated\nWhen the user reveals the task in fragments, treat early assistant responses as tentative unless they remain strictly consistent with every explicit user statement so far. Final alignment should be decided only after checking the full accumulated constraint set for consistency.\n\n### 4) Watch for anchoring and semantic drift\nIf the assistant locks onto an entity, attribute, or interpretation too early, flag anchoring risk and verify whether later user turns change the target. Track turn-by-turn drift: broadening, narrowing, swapping, or redirecting the requested object, property, or output shape is a divergence even when the response sounds reasonable.\n\n### 5) Diagnose the earliest point of departure\nIdentify the first turn where the assistant\u2019s interpretation becomes inconsistent with the user\u2019s intent or accumulated constraints. That point is usually the most actionable root cause, and later errors should be interpreted as consequences unless new evidence says otherwise.\n\n### 6) Separate surface alignment from substantive alignment\nDo not let correct vocabulary or a plausible-sounding answer hide a wrong target. An assistant can mention the right domain terms while still solving a different task, using the wrong ordering criterion, or producing the wrong output shape. Judge whether the approach actually matches the user\u2019s requested objective.\n\n### 7) Prioritize constraint mismatches over style issues\nFocus on requirement violations: scope, ordering, extremal criteria, exclusions, required fields, and target entity. Avoid spending analysis budget on phrasing, polish, or formatting unless the user explicitly requested those details. Missing columns or format choices are only violations if the user actually specified them.\n\n### 8) Treat overcommitment to unstated assumptions as a major failure mode\nIf the assistant fills in missing details that are not entailed by the conversation, mark that as divergence rather than \u201chelpfulness.\u201d This is especially important when the user has not yet revealed all constraints. Unstated assumptions should remain provisional until later user turns confirm them.\n\n### 9) Re-check every assistant move against the full cumulative spec\nIn multi-turn traces, evaluate each assistant response against the accumulated user constraints, not just the immediately preceding message. A response may be locally plausible but still wrong if it fails to integrate earlier requirements or if later user disclosures override its working interpretation.\n\n### 10) Treat later user disclosures as higher-priority constraint updates\nIf a later user message narrows, refines, or contradicts an earlier inferred interpretation, the newer user constraint wins. The analyzer should explicitly surface where the assistant failed to update its understanding, especially when the final deliverable is different from the early framing.\n\n### 11) Preserve only validated work in compacted context\nWhen preparing compacted context for follow-on analysis, keep only user-grounded constraints and assistant steps that remain valid after all revealed details. Remove speculative branches and mistaken reasoning that could anchor the next evaluation on the wrong criterion. The goal is a clean state, not a replay of every false start.\n\n### 12) Distinguish partial alignment from full correctness\nIf the assistant is moving in the right general direction but has not yet incorporated later user constraints, classify that as partial alignment rather than outright failure. Preserve reusable structure that still fits the user\u2019s objective, while isolating the exact mismatch that prevents correctness.\n\n### 13) Look for missed synthesis across fragments\nA common error in incremental disclosure is satisfying each user fragment locally while never combining them into the final complete requirement. Flag cases where the assistant handles individual shards correctly but fails to assemble them into a coherent end-to-end interpretation.\n\n### 14) Compare against the user\u2019s actual end goal, not intermediate wording\nUse the underlying requested deliverable as the anchor. Intermediate phrasing may be incomplete, misleading, or later refined. The analysis should verify that the assistant matches the final intended task scope, not merely the surface wording from an early turn.\n\n### 15) Use turn-by-turn delta analysis\nAfter each user message, update what is known, what remains unknown, and whether the assistant\u2019s current path still fits the expanded spec. This makes it easier to detect when a later turn subtly invalidates an earlier assumption and to pinpoint the exact turn where divergence begins.",
